data.content:
Here's what's happening and how to fix it:

## The Problem: C# 14 Span Overload Resolution

In .NET 10 / C# 14, new built-in span conversions cause `array.Contains(num)` inside an **expression tree** to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains<T>(IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

Expression trees **cannot represent** `Span`/`ReadOnlySpan` operations, so this throws at runtime (an `InvalidOperationException` or `NotSupportedException` when the expression tree is compiled/interpreted).

## The Fix

Force the compiler to pick the `Enumerable.Contains` overload by casting the array to `IEnumerable<int>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Use .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 3: Cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

All three prevent the compiler from choosing the span-based overload, keeping the expression tree valid.
